# gitleaks fires its generic `jwt` rule on these because a Supabase anon key IS
# a JWT. It is not a credential leak:
#
#   • The token's own payload decodes to `"role": "anon"` — NOT `service_role`.
#     It carries exactly the permissions of a signed-out visitor and is subject
#     to every RLS policy in the database.
#   • It is PUBLIC BY DESIGN. The same value is embedded verbatim in every
#     shipped client bundle (EXPO_PUBLIC_* is inlined at build time) and is
#     served to anyone who loads the web app. Verified byte-identical to the key
#     currently in .env.local.
#   • Supabase documents the anon key as publishable.
#
# Ignoring beats rewriting history here: a force-push across ~100 commits to
# scrub a value that is already public in every bundle would be pure risk for no
# security gain. Rotating it would invalidate every deployed client for the same
# non-benefit.
#
# .env.test is gitignored today (.gitignore `.env*`) and untracked; these are
# historical commits only.
#
# WHAT WOULD NOT BELONG HERE: a `service_role` key, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY,
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN, or any Supabase JWT whose payload
# says anything other than "anon". Those bypass RLS or move money — if gitleaks
# ever flags one, rotate it immediately and do not add a line to this file.
# Always decode the payload's `role` claim before assuming a hit is benign.

